       After transpose8, ri contains 8 consecutive 32-bit words? Let's think.
       Input to transpose: r0..r7 are 8 vectors, lane k of ri is element (i) of column k.
       After 8x8 transpose, lane-group becomes rows: ri holds the i-th row of 8 elements,
       i.e. words? We defined columns as the word index. 
       Lane b of word-vector xi is word i of block b.
       transpose8 treats ri as row i, columns = lanes. After transpose, ri's lanes are
       the former column i's rows... standard result: output row i contains former column i,
       i.e. output ri lane j = input rj lane i. That's the transpose.
       We want for block b: word0, word1, ... which is lane b of x0, lane b of x1, ...
       That's a column, which after transpose becomes row b. So output rb = (x0[b], x1[b], ..., x7[b]).
       YES: after transpose8(&x0..&x7), x_b contains words 0..7 of block b? 
       output rb lane j = input r_j lane b. So xb = [word0 of block b, word1 of block b, ..., word7 of block b]
       but spread across 8 lanes, and there are only 8 lanes so xb holds words 0-7 of block b. Perfect, 32 bytes.
       Then we need words 8-15 similarly in a second transpose, another 32 bytes. Block is 64 bytes = two 32-byte stores.
    */
    transpose8(&x0,&x1,&x2,&x3,&x4,&x5,&x6,&x7);
    transpose8(&x8,&x9,&x10,&x11,&x12,&x13,&x14,&x15);

    __m256i *o = (__m256i *)out;
    const __m256i *inp = (const __m256i *)in;
    /* Each block is 64 bytes = 2 ymm. Interleave low half (words 0-7) and high half (words 8-15). */
    __m256i lo[8] = {x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5,x6,x7};
    __m256i hi[8] = {x8,x9,x10,x11,x12,x13,x14,x15};
